{"format":"vetro-plugin","version":1,"manifest":{"id":"usb-ethernet","name":"USB Ethernet","version":"1.1.0","apiVersion":1,"permissions":["exec"],"author":"andrearicci93","description":"Gives a USB Ethernet adapter (Realtek RTL8152/8153/8156 and branded ones) a driver and makes it the TV's wired connection after each restart. No network use.","main":"index.js","ui":{"label":"USB Ethernet"}},"files":{"README.md":"# USB Ethernet\n\nMakes the TV use a **USB-to-Ethernet adapter** as its wired connection, and does it again after every TV restart. It is meant\nfor TVs where the adapter's light comes on but the TV never shows a wired connection, because the firmware has no driver\nloaded for it. The TV's USB ports are USB 2.0, so expect about 70 Mbit/s at most: more than many built-in ports and most\nWi-Fi links manage.\n\nIt works with Realtek **RTL8152 / RTL8153 / RTL8156** adapters and their branded versions (the USB ids that the Linux `r8152`\ndriver lists: Realtek, Microsoft, Samsung, Lenovo, TP-Link, Linksys, NVIDIA). An adapter that the TV already drives with\nits own `asix`, `ax88179_178a` or `r8152` driver is also used as the wired connection. A phone sharing its connection over\nUSB is never touched.\n\n**If your adapter does not work, open the Shard's page and make a report** (see *The report*): it shows what your TV\noffers, and is how support for other TVs and adapters grows.\n\n## Before you switch it on\n\n- **Leave the TV's own Ethernet port empty.** With a cable there, the Shard does nothing.\n- **Plug the network cable into the adapter.** Without a cable on the adapter nothing is changed and the TV stays on Wi-Fi.\n- **The TV's address changes** when it moves from Wi-Fi to the cable. After the first run, open the dashboard at the new\n  address (Settings → General → Network → Network Status on the TV, or your router's list of devices).\n- **The network drops for a few seconds** about a minute after each TV start, while the TV's network services restart.\n- **If the TV gets no address from the adapter within a minute**, the Shard puts the names back, restarts the network\n  services so that the TV returns to Wi-Fi, and stops until the next TV restart. The page says so.\n- **Wi-Fi is dropped** once the cable is up: the TV keeps one connection at a time.\n- **Switching the Shard off changes nothing until the next TV restart.** To get the built-in port back, switch it off and\n  restart the TV.\n\n## How it works\n\nOn the firmwares this was written for, the generic USB network driver (`cdc_ether`) refuses Realtek adapters by their USB\nid, and leaves them to Realtek's own driver, which is not loaded. For an adapter that has no network interface yet, the\nShard tries, in this order:\n\n1. **`cdc_ether`**, when the adapter offers the standard CDC Ethernet mode: the driver is loaded if it is not (`modprobe`,\n   else `insmod`), then told the adapter's id (`new_id`). This is the way that was tried on a real TV.\n2. **`r8152`**, when there is no CDC Ethernet mode or step 1 gave no interface: the module is loaded the same way.\n3. If neither gives an interface, it says so (*no driver on this TV can drive this adapter*) and the report shows why.\n\nSamsung's network services only treat an interface called `eth0` as the wired one, so once the adapter has an interface\nand a cable, the built-in port is renamed `ethb`, the adapter becomes `eth0`, and the network services are restarted. A TV\nrestart undoes all of it, which is why the Shard checks one minute after the TV starts and then once a minute (an adapter\nplugged in later is picked up too). It runs a setup at most three times per start of Vetro, and never two at once.\n\n## The report\n\nThe page has a **Make the report** button. The report is a text file with:\n\n- the kernel and Tizen version, and how long the TV has been up;\n- each USB device: its id (like `0bda:8153`), product name, speed, configuration, and for each interface its class and the\n  driver that has it;\n- each network interface: driver, whether it is on USB, state, cable, speed and whether it has an IPv4 address (yes or no);\n- which USB network drivers are loaded and which are on the TV, which of `modprobe`, `insmod` and `kmod` exist, and which of\n  the three network services exist;\n- the lines of Connman's settings that name interfaces;\n- the last setup's log, and the USB and network lines of the kernel log;\n- what the Shard decided (its state, the adapter it found, the driver it saw).\n\n**It leaves out** serial numbers, MAC addresses, IP addresses and the host name: any that appear in the kernel log are\nreplaced by `xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx` and `x.x.x.x`. Nothing is sent anywhere. You read it on the page, then **save it as a\nfile** or **copy** it, and post it where you ask for help (if the dashboard blocks downloads, copying always works). You can\ntype the TV's model into the page first (it is printed on the back); it is added at the top of the report.\n\n## Permissions\n\n`exec`: loading a driver, binding a USB device, renaming network interfaces and restarting the TV's network services need root.\n\n## Commands it runs\n\nAll of them are in [`tv.sh`](tv.sh), sent to the TV's root agent as one script.\n\n| Command | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| `tv.sh check`: reads `/sys/bus/usb/devices/*` (`idVendor`, `idProduct`, interface classes), `/sys/class/net/*` (`device`, `driver`, `carrier`), `ip -4 -o addr show`, `/proc/uptime`, a lock file in `/tmp` | to find the adapter, its interface and whether it has a cable and an address. Reads only. Every minute, 8 s timeout |\n| `tv.sh report`: the same, plus `/proc/modules`, `/usr/lib/modules/<kernel>/kernel/drivers/net/usb`, `/etc/tizen-release`, `/etc/connman/main.conf`, `ip route`, `command -v`, `systemctl cat`, `dmesg` | the text of the report. Reads only, only when you press the button |\n| `tv.sh apply`, run in the background (`setsid`), output in `/tmp/vetro-usb-ethernet.log`, a lock directory in `/tmp` | only when the check finds an adapter that is not set up yet and the built-in port has no cable: |\n| `modprobe cdc_ether` (or `insmod` of its `.ko`, and of `usbnet.ko` if that is not loaded) | the generic driver, when it is not loaded. When the TV has no `modprobe` or `insmod`, a symbolic link called `insmod` to `kmod` is made in `/tmp/vetro-usb-ethernet.bin` |\n| `echo \"<vendor> <product> 02 0000 0000\" > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/cdc_ether/new_id` | lets the generic driver take the adapter |\n| `modprobe r8152` (or `insmod` of its `.ko`) | Realtek's driver, when the generic one gave no interface |\n| `ip link set <port> up`, only for the adapter, or for the built-in port when it is down | a port that is down cannot say whether it has a cable; without a clear \"no cable\" nothing is renamed |\n| `ip link set eth0 name ethb`, `ip link set <adapter> name eth0` (each after `ip link set … down`) | the built-in port gives its name to the adapter |\n| `systemctl restart connman.service`, then `systemctl restart vd-net-config.service net-config.service` (each only if it exists) | Samsung's network services pick up the new `eth0` |\n| the same renames the other way, and the same restarts, once, if there is no address after a minute with a cable | putting the TV back on its own connection |\n| `tail -n 1` / `cat /tmp/vetro-usb-ethernet.log` | the result of the last run, on this Shard's page |\n\n## Network\n\nNone. It sends nothing anywhere.\n\n## Data it keeps\n\nNone. It does not use `ctx.config`. The log of the last run, the lock, the marker of a rollback and, when the TV has no `insmod`,\nthe `insmod` link to `kmod` (`/tmp/vetro-usb-ethernet.bin`) are in `/tmp` on the TV and go away at the next restart.\n\n## Tested\n\n**Tried on a real TV:** an AU7000 (2021, Tizen 6.0) with an RTL8153 adapter, with version 1.0.0, which did this: `new_id`\nfor the generic driver, the renames and the restarts. After a TV restart the Shard set it up on its own, the adapter became\nthe TV's wired connection with an address and the default route, and Network Status showed Ethernet. The steps above are the\nsame, and the automated test replays that case and checks that the commands are the same, plus bringing the adapter up to read\nits cable.\n\n**Not tried on a real TV yet:** 1.1.0 itself; loading `cdc_ether` or `r8152` when the TV has not loaded them; the `r8152`\nroute; adapters other than that RTL8153; other TVs, other Tizen versions; the rollback. They are tested only against a\nsimulated TV (`tests/usb-ethernet.test.cjs`: a fake `/sys` and fake `ip`, `systemctl` and `modprobe`). If you try it, the\nreport is the most useful thing you can send, whether it worked or not.\n","index.js":"'use strict';\n// USB Ethernet: lets the TV use a USB-to-Ethernet adapter as its wired connection. Plain ES5: older TVs run Node 4.4.\n//\n// Realtek RTL8152/8153/8156 adapters (and their branded versions) get no driver on some Samsung firmwares: the generic\n// cdc_ether driver refuses them by their USB id and Realtek's own driver is not loaded. This Shard gives the adapter a\n// driver (cdc_ether told the id again, else r8152), then, because Samsung's network services only treat eth0 as \"Wired\",\n// renames the empty built-in port ethb and the adapter eth0, and restarts the services. All of it is lost at a TV restart,\n// which is why the Shard does it again after every boot. If the TV gets no address from the adapter, the names go back.\n// The commands are in tv.sh (README: Commands it runs). The page can also produce a report, to see why an adapter does not work.\n\nvar fs = require('fs');\nvar path = require('path');\n\nvar VERSION = '1.1.0';\n\n// What a finished setup (the last line of tv.sh's log) means for the number of runs this start of Vetro may spend.\nvar COUNTED = { 'done': true, 'no-address': true, 'rename-failed': true, 'reverted': true, 'no-driver': true };\n\nmodule.exports = function (ctx) {\n    var FIRST_CHECK_MS = 20000;      // Vetro has just started: give the root agent a moment\n    var RETRY_MS = 20000;            // the agent is not connected yet\n    var POLL_MS = 60000;             // an adapter plugged in later, or a cable moved\n    var APPLY_POLL_MS = 3000;        // while a setup runs, how often to ask whether it has finished\n    var APPLY_MAX_MS = 180000;       // a setup takes two minutes at the very most\n    var MIN_TV_UPTIME_S = 60;        // let Samsung's own network start finish first\n    var MAX_APPLIES = 3;             // per Vetro start: never restart the network in a loop\n    var REPORT_MS = 30000;\n\n    var timer = null, stopped = false, busy = false, applies = 0, unaddressed = 0, reporting = false;\n    var last = { state: 'starting', iface: null, carrier: null, address: null, adapter: null, driver: null, result: null, resultAt: null, error: null };\n\n    function script() { return fs.readFileSync(path.join(ctx.dir, 'tv.sh'), 'utf8'); }\n    function b64(text) {\n        var hasFrom = typeof Buffer.from === 'function' && Buffer.from !== Uint8Array.from;   // Node 4.4 has only the TypedArray one\n        return (hasFrom ? Buffer.from(text, 'utf8') : new Buffer(text, 'utf8')).toString('base64');\n    }\n    function run(mode) { return 'sh -c \"$(echo ' + b64(script()) + ' | base64 -d)\" vetro-usb-ethernet ' + mode; }\n    function out(r) { return String((r && r.stdout) || '').trim(); }\n\n    function later(ms) {\n        if (stopped) return;\n        clearTimeout(timer);\n        timer = setTimeout(tick, ms);\n    }\n\n    function parse(text) {\n        var o = {};\n        text.split(/\\s+/).forEach(function (kv) {\n            var i = kv.indexOf('=');\n            if (i > 0) o[kv.slice(0, i)] = kv.slice(i + 1);\n        });\n        return o;\n    }\n\n    function check() {\n        return ctx.execute(run('check'), 8000).then(function (r) {\n            var o = parse(out(r));\n            if (!o.state) throw new Error('No answer from the TV');\n            return o;\n        });\n    }\n\n    function remember(o) {\n        last.error = null;\n        last.state = o.state;\n        last.iface = !o.iface || o.iface === '-' ? null : o.iface;\n        last.carrier = o.carrier === '1' ? true : o.carrier === '0' ? false : null;\n        last.address = o.ip === 'yes' ? true : o.ip === 'no' ? false : null;\n        last.adapter = o.adapter || null;\n        last.driver = !o.driver || o.driver === '-' ? null : o.driver;\n    }\n\n    function readResult() {\n        return ctx.execute('tail -n 1 /tmp/vetro-usb-ethernet.log 2>/dev/null', 5000).then(function (r) {\n            var m = /^result=([a-z-]+)$/.exec(out(r));\n            last.result = m ? m[1] : 'unknown';\n            last.resultAt = Date.now();\n            return last.result;\n        });\n    }\n\n    // Starts the setup on the TV, detached (it restarts the network services, which cuts this very connection for a moment),\n    // then asks every few seconds whether it has finished, and reads how it ended.\n    function apply() {\n        last.state = 'applying';\n        var started = Date.now();\n        var cmd = 'setsid ' + run('apply') + ' >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null & echo started';\n        function waitDone() {\n            if (stopped) return Promise.resolve();\n            return new Promise(function (resolve) { setTimeout(resolve, APPLY_POLL_MS); }).then(function () {\n                if (stopped) return;\n                return check().then(function (o) { return o.state === 'applying'; }, function () { return true; }).then(function (again) {\n                    if (again && Date.now() - started < APPLY_MAX_MS) return waitDone();\n                });\n            });\n        }\n        return ctx.execute(cmd, 10000).then(waitDone).then(function () {\n            if (!stopped) return readResult();\n        }).then(function (result) {\n            if (result && COUNTED[result]) applies++;\n            last.state = 'checking';\n        });\n    }\n\n    function tick() {\n        if (stopped || busy) return;\n        busy = true;\n        var next = POLL_MS;\n        check().then(function (o) {\n            remember(o);\n            // An adapter plugged in again after the swap comes back as eth0 by itself; if the TV gives it no address\n            // in two checks in a row, the network services are restarted for it.\n            unaddressed = o.state === 'in-place' && last.carrier === true && last.address === false ? unaddressed + 1 : 0;\n            var needs = o.state === 'bind' || o.state === 'swap' || o.state === 'builtin-unknown' || unaddressed >= 2;\n            if (!needs) return;\n            if (Number(o.up) < MIN_TV_UPTIME_S) { next = RETRY_MS; return; }\n            if (applies >= MAX_APPLIES) { last.state = 'gave-up'; return; }\n            unaddressed = 0;\n            return apply().then(function () { next = 1000; });   // check again right after, to show the new state\n        }).catch(function (e) {\n            last.error = String(e && e.message || e).slice(0, 200);\n            next = RETRY_MS;\n        }).then(function () {\n            busy = false;\n            later(next);\n        });\n    }\n\n    var page = null;\n    function uiPage() {\n        if (page) return page;\n        var places = [path.join(__dirname, 'ui.html'), path.join(ctx.dir || '', 'ui.html')];\n        for (var i = 0; i < places.length; i++) {\n            try { page = fs.readFileSync(places[i], 'utf8'); return page; } catch (e) { /* next */ }\n        }\n        return '<!doctype html><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><p>ui.html is missing from the Shard folder: reinstall this Shard.</p>';\n    }\n\n    function json(res, body, code) {\n        res.writeHead(code || 200, { 'content-type': 'application/json', 'cache-control': 'no-store' });\n        res.end(JSON.stringify(body));\n    }\n\n    // What the person sends along when an adapter does not work: see README, \"The report\".\n    function makeReport() {\n        return ctx.execute(run('report'), REPORT_MS).then(function (r) {\n            var header = 'USB Ethernet report\\nShard version: ' + VERSION + '\\nGenerated: ' + new Date().toISOString() + ' (the TV’s clock)\\n' +\n                'Shard state: ' + JSON.stringify(last) + '\\n\\n';\n            return header + out(r) + '\\n';\n        });\n    }\n\n    return {\n        start: function () { stopped = false; applies = 0; unaddressed = 0; later(FIRST_CHECK_MS); },\n        // Switching the Shard off changes nothing on the TV: the names and the driver stay as they are until the next restart.\n        stop: function () { stopped = true; clearTimeout(timer); timer = null; },\n        status: function () { return { state: last.state, result: last.result, error: last.error }; },\n        route: function (req, res, sub) {\n            if (sub === 'ui' && req.method === 'GET') {\n                res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html; charset=utf-8', 'cache-control': 'no-store' });\n                res.end(uiPage());\n                return true;\n            }\n            if (sub === 'state' && req.method === 'GET') { json(res, last); return true; }\n            if (sub === 'log' && req.method === 'GET') {\n                ctx.execute('cat /tmp/vetro-usb-ethernet.log 2>/dev/null', 5000).then(function (r) {\n                    json(res, { log: out(r) });\n                }).catch(function (e) { json(res, { error: String(e && e.message || e) }, 503); });\n                return true;\n            }\n            if (sub === 'report' && req.method === 'GET') {\n                if (reporting) { json(res, { error: 'A report is already being made' }, 409); return true; }\n                reporting = true;\n                makeReport().then(function (text) {\n                    reporting = false;\n                    json(res, { text: text });\n                }, function (e) {\n                    reporting = false;\n                    json(res, { error: String(e && e.message || e) }, 503);\n                });\n                return true;\n            }\n            return false;\n        }\n    };\n};\nmodule.exports.VERSION = VERSION;\n","tv.sh":"# Runs on the TV as root. $1: check (reads only), report (reads only, for people) or apply (loads a driver, renames, restarts\n# the network services). VETRO_USB_ROOT and VETRO_USB_TMP are for the Shard's tests only: unset, everything is the real TV.\nmode=$1\nR=${VETRO_USB_ROOT:-}\nT=${VETRO_USB_TMP:-/tmp}\nUSB=$R/sys/bus/usb/devices\nNET=$R/sys/class/net\nLOG=$T/vetro-usb-ethernet.log\nLOCK=$T/vetro-usb-ethernet.lock\nMARK=$T/vetro-usb-ethernet.reverted\n# USB ids that the Linux r8152 driver lists for Realtek RTL8152/8153/8156 adapters and their branded versions.\nIDS=\" 0bda:8050 0bda:8152 0bda:8153 0bda:8155 0bda:8156 045e:07ab 045e:07c6 04e8:a101 0955:09ff 13b1:0041 17ef:304f 17ef:3052 17ef:3054 17ef:3057 17ef:3062 17ef:3069 17ef:3082 17ef:3098 17ef:7205 17ef:720a 17ef:720b 17ef:720c 17ef:7214 17ef:721e 17ef:8153 17ef:a359 17ef:a387 2357:0601 \"\n# Network drivers that are always an Ethernet adapter. cdc_ether, rndis_host, cdc_ncm and ipheth are also phones and tethering.\nDRIVERS=\" r8152 asix ax88179_178a \"\n\nadapter=; vid=; pid=; iface=; drv=; st=none; ecm=no\n\nrd() { cat \"$1\" 2>/dev/null; }\nhas_ip() { ip -4 -o addr show dev \"$1\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q inet; }\nunit_exists() { [ -e \"$R/usr/lib/systemd/system/$1.service\" ] || [ -e \"$R/lib/systemd/system/$1.service\" ] || systemctl cat \"$1.service\" >/dev/null 2>&1; }\nin_ids() { case \"$IDS\" in *\" $1 \"*) return 0;; esac; return 1; }\nlogline() { echo \"$(date '+%H:%M:%S') $*\"; }\n\n# Finds the adapter: first by its network interface (driver one of DRIVERS, or cdc_ether with an id from IDS), then by the\n# USB id alone (an adapter without a driver yet). Sets adapter vid pid iface drv.\nscan() {\n  adapter=; vid=; pid=; iface=; drv=\n  for nd in \"$NET\"/*; do\n    [ -e \"$nd/device\" ] || continue\n    dev=$(readlink -f \"$nd/device\" 2>/dev/null)\n    case \"$dev\" in */usb*|*/[0-9]*-[0-9]*:*) ;; *) continue;; esac\n    base=${dev##*/}; bus=${base%%:*}\n    [ -f \"$USB/$bus/idVendor\" ] || continue\n    d=$(basename \"$(readlink -f \"$nd/device/driver\" 2>/dev/null)\" 2>/dev/null)\n    v=$(rd \"$USB/$bus/idVendor\"); p=$(rd \"$USB/$bus/idProduct\")\n    ok=no\n    case \"$DRIVERS\" in *\" $d \"*) ok=yes;; esac\n    [ \"$d\" = cdc_ether ] && in_ids \"$v:$p\" && ok=yes\n    # The TV's own port may itself be a USB chip (asix): an eth0 that is not on a known Realtek id is the adapter only after the swap.\n    [ \"${nd##*/}\" = eth0 ] && [ ! -e \"$NET/ethb\" ] && ! in_ids \"$v:$p\" && ok=no\n    if [ $ok = yes ]; then adapter=$bus; vid=$v; pid=$p; iface=${nd##*/}; drv=$d; return; fi\n  done\n  for d in \"$USB\"/*; do\n    case \"${d##*/}\" in *:*) continue;; esac\n    [ -f \"$d/idVendor\" ] || continue\n    v=$(rd \"$d/idVendor\"); p=$(rd \"$d/idProduct\")\n    if in_ids \"$v:$p\"; then adapter=${d##*/}; vid=$v; pid=$p; return; fi\n  done\n}\n\n# Sets st (and ecm) from what scan found.\ndecide() {\n  st=none; ecm=no\n  [ -n \"$adapter\" ] || return\n  for i in \"$USB/$adapter\":*; do\n    [ \"$(rd \"$i/bInterfaceClass\")$(rd \"$i/bInterfaceSubClass\")\" = 0206 ] && ecm=yes\n  done\n  if [ -z \"$iface\" ]; then st=bind; return; fi\n  if [ \"$iface\" = eth0 ]; then st=in-place; return; fi\n  c=$(rd \"$NET/$iface/carrier\")\n  if [ ! -e \"$NET/eth0\" ]; then\n    case \"$c\" in 0) st=no-cable;; *) st=swap;; esac\n    return\n  fi\n  # The built-in port must read \"no cable\" (0). A cable (1) or an unreadable value keeps it as it is.\n  b=$(rd \"$NET/eth0/carrier\")\n  case \"$b\" in\n    0) case \"$c\" in 0) st=no-cable;; *) st=swap;; esac;;\n    1) st=builtin-cable;;\n    *) st=builtin-unknown;;\n  esac\n}\n\nlock_alive() { [ -d \"$LOCK\" ] && kill -0 \"$(rd \"$LOCK/pid\")\" 2>/dev/null; }\n\nif [ \"$mode\" = check ]; then\n  scan; decide\n  [ -e \"$MARK\" ] && st=reverted\n  lock_alive && st=applying\n  c=-; a=-\n  if [ -n \"$iface\" ]; then\n    c=$(rd \"$NET/$iface/carrier\"); c=${c:-0}\n    if has_ip \"$iface\"; then a=yes; else a=no; fi\n  fi\n  echo \"state=$st iface=${iface:--} carrier=$c ip=$a adapter=${vid:+$vid:$pid} driver=${drv:--} up=$(cut -d. -f1 \"$R/proc/uptime\")\"\n  exit 0\nfi\n\nif [ \"$mode\" = report ]; then\n  # Everything below is read-only. No serial number, no MAC address, no IP address, no host name: addresses that slip in\n  # through the kernel log are replaced at the end.\n  {\n    echo \"== TV\"\n    echo \"kernel: $(uname -r) $(uname -m)\"\n    head -n 6 \"$R/etc/tizen-release\" 2>/dev/null\n    echo \"uptime_s: $(cut -d. -f1 \"$R/proc/uptime\")\"\n    echo \"== USB devices\"\n    for d in \"$USB\"/*; do\n      case \"${d##*/}\" in *:*) continue;; esac\n      [ -f \"$d/idVendor\" ] || continue\n      echo \"- ${d##*/} id=$(rd \"$d/idVendor\"):$(rd \"$d/idProduct\") product=\\\"$(rd \"$d/product\")\\\" speed=$(rd \"$d/speed\") config=$(rd \"$d/bConfigurationValue\")/$(rd \"$d/bNumConfigurations\")\"\n      for i in \"$USB/${d##*/}\":*; do\n        [ -e \"$i\" ] || continue\n        dr=$(basename \"$(readlink -f \"$i/driver\" 2>/dev/null)\" 2>/dev/null); [ -e \"$i/driver\" ] || dr=-\n        echo \"    ${i##*/} class=$(rd \"$i/bInterfaceClass\")/$(rd \"$i/bInterfaceSubClass\")/$(rd \"$i/bInterfaceProtocol\") driver=$dr\"\n      done\n    done\n    echo \"== network interfaces (no addresses)\"\n    for n in \"$NET\"/*; do\n      [ -e \"$n\" ] || continue\n      dr=-; [ -e \"$n/device/driver\" ] && dr=$(basename \"$(readlink -f \"$n/device/driver\" 2>/dev/null)\" 2>/dev/null)\n      bus=no; case \"$(readlink -f \"$n/device\" 2>/dev/null)\" in */usb*|*/[0-9]*-[0-9]*:*) bus=usb;; esac\n      ip4=no; has_ip \"${n##*/}\" && ip4=yes\n      echo \"- ${n##*/} driver=$dr bus=$bus state=$(rd \"$n/operstate\") carrier=$(rd \"$n/carrier\") speed=$(rd \"$n/speed\") ipv4=$ip4\"\n    done\n    echo \"default route via: $(ip route 2>/dev/null | awk '/^default/ { for (i = 1; i < NF; i++) if ($i == \"dev\") print $(i + 1) }' | sort -u | tr '\\n' ' ')\"\n    echo \"== USB network modules\"\n    echo \"loaded: $(awk '{print $1}' \"$R/proc/modules\" 2>/dev/null | grep -E '^(usbnet|cdc_|asix|ax88|r815|rndis|ipheth|mii)' | tr '\\n' ' ')\"\n    echo \"available: $(ls \"$R/usr/lib/modules/$(uname -r)/kernel/drivers/net/usb\" 2>/dev/null | tr '\\n' ' ')\"\n    echo \"== tools and units\"\n    for c in modprobe insmod kmod setsid systemctl ip dmesg; do\n      if command -v \"$c\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo \"$c: yes\"; else echo \"$c: no\"; fi\n    done\n    for u in connman vd-net-config net-config; do\n      if unit_exists \"$u\"; then echo \"$u.service: yes\"; else echo \"$u.service: no\"; fi\n    done\n    echo \"== connman main.conf\"\n    grep -E '^(NetworkInterfaceBlacklist|NetworkCellularInterfaceList|SingleConnectedTechnology)' \"$R/etc/connman/main.conf\" 2>/dev/null\n    echo \"== what the Shard sees\"\n    scan; decide\n    [ -e \"$MARK\" ] && st=reverted\n    lock_alive && st=applying\n    echo \"state=$st adapter=${vid:+$vid:$pid} iface=${iface:--} driver=${drv:--} ecm_interface=$ecm\"\n    echo \"== last setup log\"\n    tail -n 40 \"$LOG\" 2>/dev/null\n    echo \"== kernel log (usb and network lines)\"\n    dmesg 2>/dev/null | grep -i -E 'usb|cdc|r8152|asix|ax88|rtl81|eth[0-9b]|link (is|becomes)' | tail -n 60\n  } 2>&1 | sed -e 's/[0-9]\\{1,3\\}\\(\\.[0-9]\\{1,3\\}\\)\\{3\\}/x.x.x.x/g' \\\n               -e 's/\\([0-9A-Fa-f]\\{2\\}:\\)\\{5\\}[0-9A-Fa-f]\\{2\\}/xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx/g'\n  exit 0\nfi\n\n[ \"$mode\" = apply ] || exit 2\n\n# ---- apply ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# Only one setup at a time: a run takes up to two minutes and the Shard looks again every minute.\nif ! mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then\n  if lock_alive; then echo \"result=busy\"; exit 0; fi\n  rm -rf \"$LOCK\"; mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null || { echo \"result=busy\"; exit 0; }\nfi\necho $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$LOCK\"' EXIT\n# After a rollback nothing is tried again until the TV restarts; the log of the rollback stays as it is.\nif [ -e \"$MARK\" ]; then echo \"result=reverted\"; exit 0; fi\nexec > \"$LOG\" 2>&1\ndate\n\n# Loads a kernel module (and usbnet, which cdc_ether needs). modprobe, else insmod, else kmod under the name insmod.\nload_mod() {\n  [ -d \"$R/sys/module/$1\" ] && return 0\n  if command -v modprobe >/dev/null 2>&1 && modprobe \"$1\" 2>/dev/null; then echo \"loaded $1 with modprobe\"; return 0; fi\n  ko=$(find \"$R/usr/lib/modules/$(uname -r)\" -name \"$1.ko\" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)\n  [ -n \"$ko\" ] || { echo \"no $1.ko on this TV\"; return 1; }\n  [ \"$1\" = cdc_ether ] && [ ! -d \"$R/sys/module/usbnet\" ] && load_mod usbnet\n  if command -v insmod >/dev/null 2>&1; then ins=insmod\n  elif command -v kmod >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    mkdir -p \"$T/vetro-usb-ethernet.bin\" && ln -sf \"$(command -v kmod)\" \"$T/vetro-usb-ethernet.bin/insmod\" && ins=$T/vetro-usb-ethernet.bin/insmod\n  else echo \"no modprobe, insmod or kmod\"; return 1; fi\n  if $ins \"$ko\"; then echo \"loaded $1 with ${ins##*/}\"; return 0; fi\n  echo \"could not load $1\"; return 1\n}\n\n# Waits for the adapter to get a network interface ($1 seconds at most).\nwait_iface() {\n  n=0\n  while [ \"$n\" -lt \"$1\" ]; do\n    scan; [ -n \"$iface\" ] && return 0\n    sleep 1; n=$((n + 1))\n  done\n  scan; [ -n \"$iface\" ]\n}\n\nrestart_units() {\n  if unit_exists connman; then systemctl restart connman.service; echo \"connman rc=$?\"; else echo \"connman: no unit\"; fi\n  sleep 8\n  for u in vd-net-config net-config; do\n    if unit_exists \"$u\"; then systemctl restart \"$u.service\"; echo \"$u rc=$?\"; else echo \"$u: no unit\"; fi\n  done\n}\n\nscan; decide\necho \"start: state=$st adapter=${vid:+$vid:$pid} iface=${iface:--} driver=${drv:--} ecm=$ecm\"\n\n# 1. A driver for the adapter. The generic cdc_ether driver first (the way that is known to work), then Realtek's r8152.\nif [ $st = bind ]; then\n  if [ $ecm = yes ]; then\n    if load_mod cdc_ether && [ -d \"$R/sys/bus/usb/drivers/cdc_ether\" ]; then\n      echo \"$vid $pid 02 0000 0000\" > \"$R/sys/bus/usb/drivers/cdc_ether/new_id\"\n      echo \"new_id rc=$?\"\n      wait_iface 10 && echo \"cdc_ether took it as $iface\"\n    fi\n  fi\n  if [ -z \"$iface\" ]; then\n    if load_mod r8152; then wait_iface 15 && echo \"r8152 took it as $iface\"; fi\n  fi\n  if [ -z \"$iface\" ]; then echo \"result=no-driver\"; exit 0; fi\n  decide\n  echo \"after the driver: state=$st iface=${iface:--} driver=${drv:--}\"\nfi\n\n# 2. A built-in port that is down cannot say whether it has a cable: bring it up and read again.\nif [ $st = builtin-unknown ]; then\n  ip link set eth0 up; sleep 3; scan; decide\n  echo \"built-in port up: state=$st\"\nfi\n\n# 3. The adapter needs a cable before anything is renamed: without one the TV keeps the connection it has.\nif [ $st = swap ] || [ $st = no-cable ]; then\n  ip link set \"$iface\" up\n  n=0\n  while [ \"$(rd \"$NET/$iface/carrier\")\" != 1 ] && [ $n -lt 8 ]; do sleep 1; n=$((n + 1)); done\n  if [ \"$(rd \"$NET/$iface/carrier\")\" = 1 ]; then st=swap; else st=no-cable; fi\nfi\nif [ $st = no-cable ]; then echo \"result=no-cable\"; exit 0; fi\nif [ $st != swap ] && [ $st != in-place ]; then echo \"result=$st\"; exit 0; fi\n\n# 4. The built-in port gives its name to the adapter (Samsung's network services only treat eth0 as the wired one).\norig=$iface; hadb=no\nif [ $st = swap ]; then\n  if [ -e \"$NET/eth0\" ]; then\n    ip link set eth0 down\n    ip link set eth0 name ethb || { ip link set eth0 up; echo \"result=rename-failed\"; exit 1; }\n    hadb=yes\n  fi\n  ip link set \"$iface\" down\n  if ! ip link set \"$iface\" name eth0; then\n    ip link set \"$iface\" up\n    [ $hadb = yes ] && ip link set ethb name eth0 && ip link set eth0 up\n    echo \"result=rename-failed\"; exit 1\n  fi\n  echo \"renamed: $(ls \"$NET\" | tr '\\n' ' ')\"\nelif has_ip eth0 || [ \"$(rd \"$NET/eth0/carrier\")\" != 1 ]; then\n  # In place already (an adapter plugged in again gets eth0 by itself): restart only if it has a cable and no address.\n  echo \"result=nothing-to-do\"; exit 0\nfi\n\nrestart_units\n\n# 5. An address within a minute, or everything goes back (the TV must not be left without a network).\nn=0\nwhile [ $n -lt 30 ]; do\n  has_ip eth0 && break\n  sleep 2; n=$((n + 1))\ndone\necho \"carrier=$(rd \"$NET/eth0/carrier\")\"\nif has_ip eth0; then echo \"route via: $(ip route 2>/dev/null | awk '/^default/ { for (i = 1; i < NF; i++) if ($i == \"dev\") print $(i + 1) }' | sort -u | tr '\\n' ' ')\"; echo \"result=done\"; exit 0; fi\nif [ \"$(rd \"$NET/eth0/carrier\")\" = 1 ] && [ $st = swap ]; then\n  echo \"no address after a minute with a cable: putting the names back\"\n  ip link set eth0 down\n  ip link set eth0 name \"$orig\"\n  if [ $hadb = yes ]; then ip link set ethb name eth0; ip link set eth0 up; fi\n  : > \"$MARK\"\n  restart_units\n  echo \"result=reverted\"; exit 0\nfi\necho \"result=no-address\"\n","ui.html":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"light dark\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n<title>USB Ethernet</title>\n<style>\nbody { font: 16px/1.45 system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 24px; max-width: 42em; }\nh2 { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.2; }\nh3 { margin: 28px 0 6px; font-size: 1.1rem; }\ndt { font-weight: 600; margin-top: 10px; }\ndd { margin: 2px 0 0; }\np, .small { margin: 8px 0; }\n.small { font-size: 14px; opacity: .8; }\npre, textarea { box-sizing: border-box; width: 100%; white-space: pre-wrap; font: 13px ui-monospace, monospace; padding: 12px; border: 1px solid #8884; border-radius: 8px; background: transparent; color: inherit; }\ntextarea { height: 18em; resize: vertical; }\ninput[type=text] { font: inherit; padding: 6px 8px; width: 18em; max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #8886; border-radius: 6px; background: transparent; color: inherit; }\nbutton { font: inherit; padding: 8px 16px; margin: 0 8px 8px 0; }\n.bad { color: #c0392b; }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h2>USB Ethernet</h2>\n<dl id=\"s\"><dd>Reading…</dd></dl>\n<p><button id=\"b\">Show the last setup</button></p>\n<pre id=\"log\" hidden></pre>\n\n<h3>Report</h3>\n<p class=\"small\">If your adapter does not work, a report tells us what this TV offers: its USB devices, network interfaces, drivers, and the last setup log. It holds no serial numbers, MAC addresses, IP addresses or host name, and nothing is sent anywhere: you read it here, then save it or copy it and post it where you ask for help.</p>\n<p><label for=\"model\">Your TV&rsquo;s model (printed on the back, like UE50AU7000), added to the report:</label><br><input type=\"text\" id=\"model\" maxlength=\"40\" autocomplete=\"off\"></p>\n<p><button id=\"make\">Make the report</button></p>\n<p class=\"small bad\" id=\"reportMsg\"></p>\n<div id=\"reportBox\" hidden>\n  <textarea id=\"report\" readonly spellcheck=\"false\" aria-label=\"Report\"></textarea>\n  <p><button id=\"save\">Save as a file</button><button id=\"copy\">Copy</button></p>\n  <p class=\"small\">If saving does nothing (the dashboard may block downloads), use Copy, or select the text above.</p>\n</div>\n\n<script>\nvar WORDS = {\n  starting: 'Waiting for the TV',\n  checking: 'Checking the result',\n  none: 'No supported USB Ethernet adapter plugged in',\n  bind: 'Adapter found, not set up yet',\n  swap: 'Adapter set up, waiting to become the wired connection',\n  'no-cable': 'Adapter ready: plug a network cable into it',\n  'builtin-unknown': 'Not used: the TV could not say whether its own Ethernet port has a cable',\n  'builtin-cable': 'Not used: the TV’s own Ethernet port has a cable',\n  'in-place': 'The adapter is the TV’s wired connection',\n  applying: 'Setting up (the network drops for a few seconds)',\n  reverted: 'The TV got no address from the adapter, so the old settings were put back. Restart the TV to try again',\n  'gave-up': 'Stopped after 3 tries: restart the TV to try again'\n};\nvar RESULTS = {\n  'done': 'done',\n  'no-driver': 'no driver on this TV can drive this adapter (make a report)',\n  'no-address': 'set up, but no address yet',\n  'reverted': 'the old settings were put back',\n  'rename-failed': 'the TV refused to rename the network interfaces',\n  'no-cable': 'waiting for a cable',\n  'nothing-to-do': 'nothing to do',\n  'builtin-cable': 'the TV’s own port has a cable',\n  'busy': 'another setup was running'\n};\nfunction $(id) { return document.getElementById(id); }\nfunction row(k, v) { return '<dt>' + k + '</dt><dd>' + v + '</dd>'; }\nfunction yn(v) { return v === true ? 'yes' : v === false ? 'no' : '—'; }\nfunction esc(t) { return String(t).replace(/[&<>\"]/g, function (c) { return { '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '\"': '&quot;' }[c]; }); }\nfunction show(d) {\n  var html = row('State', esc(WORDS[d.state] || d.state));\n  html += row('Adapter', d.adapter ? esc(d.adapter) + (d.driver ? ' (driver ' + esc(d.driver) + ')' : '') : '—');\n  html += row('Interface', esc(d.iface || '—')) + row('Cable connected', yn(d.carrier)) + row('Has an address', yn(d.address));\n  html += row('Last setup', d.result ? esc(RESULTS[d.result] || d.result) + ' (' + new Date(d.resultAt).toLocaleTimeString() + ')' : 'not run since Vetro started');\n  if (d.error) html += row('Error', esc(d.error));\n  $('s').innerHTML = html;\n}\nfunction get() { fetch('state').then(function (r) { return r.json(); }).then(show).catch(function () {}); }\n$('b').onclick = function () {\n  fetch('log').then(function (r) { return r.json(); }).then(function (d) {\n    var p = $('log'); p.hidden = false; p.textContent = d.log || d.error || 'No setup yet since the TV started';\n  }).catch(function () {});\n};\n\nvar made = '';\nfunction modelLine() {\n  var m = $('model').value.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9 ._\\/-]/g, '').slice(0, 40);\n  return m ? 'TV model (typed by the user): ' + m + '\\n' : '';\n}\n$('make').onclick = function () {\n  var btn = $('make');\n  btn.disabled = true; $('reportMsg').textContent = 'Reading the TV…';\n  fetch('report').then(function (r) { return r.json(); }).then(function (d) {\n    btn.disabled = false;\n    if (!d.text) { $('reportMsg').textContent = d.error || 'No report'; return; }\n    $('reportMsg').textContent = '';\n    made = d.text;\n    $('report').value = modelLine() + made;\n    $('reportBox').hidden = false;\n  }).catch(function (e) { btn.disabled = false; $('reportMsg').textContent = 'Could not make the report: ' + e.message; });\n};\n$('model').oninput = function () { if (made) $('report').value = modelLine() + made; };\n$('save').onclick = function () {\n  var text = $('report').value;\n  var name = 'usb-ethernet-report' + ($('model').value.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9._-]/g, '') ? '-' + $('model').value.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9._-]/g, '') : '') + '.txt';\n  try {\n    var url = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([text], { type: 'text/plain' }));\n    var a = document.createElement('a');\n    a.href = url; a.download = name; document.body.appendChild(a); a.click(); document.body.removeChild(a);\n    setTimeout(function () { URL.revokeObjectURL(url); }, 5000);\n  } catch (e) { $('reportMsg').textContent = 'Saving is blocked here: use Copy.'; }\n};\n$('copy').onclick = function () {\n  var t = $('report'); t.focus(); t.select();\n  var ok = false;\n  try { ok = document.execCommand('copy'); } catch (e) { ok = false; }\n  $('reportMsg').textContent = ok ? '' : 'Copying is blocked here: the text is selected, press Ctrl+C.';\n};\nget(); setInterval(get, 3000);\n</script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}}
